      "prompt": "Give me one short-answer application question about changing that module's storage implementation while preserving its public interface. Do not show the answer.",
      "response": "Here's a short-answer question for you:\n\n---\n\nA course app keeps student records in an in-memory `Map<String, List<Integer>>`. The registrar now requires the records to move into a SQLite database. The public interface today is:\n\n```java\nclass StudentRecords {\n    public double getGpa(String id)            { ... }\n    public String formatTranscript(String id)  { ... }\n    public void   recordScore(String id, int s){ ... }\n    public Map<String, List<Integer>> getRecords() { ... }   // added \"for convenience\"\n}\n```\n\nThree clients exist:\n\n- `ReportBuilder` calls only `formatTranscript(id)`\n- `GpaWidget` calls only `getGpa(id)`\n- `BackupTool` calls `getRecords()`, iterates the map, and writes each entry to a file\n\n**Your task:** work through this storage swap. In a short answer (three to five sentences, or a few bullet lines), state:\n\n1. Which of the four public operations would you change, and what would the new version look like?\n2. Which client files would have to be edited, and why?\n3. What does your count of edited modules tell you about whether this class actually hides the storage decision?\n\nAnswer in terms of this scenario only — no need to define information hiding in general.",
